findings.failure_scenario:
MEASURED SURVIVING MUTATION. In ConsumerClient.bytesOf (client/src/main/java/binjava/client/ConsumerClient.java:119-129) swap the two guards so the source wins whenever one is configured:

    private byte[] bytesOf(Delivery delivery) throws IOException {
        if (segmentSource == null) {
            if (delivery.via() != FetchMode.DIRECT) {
                return delivery.segment();
            }
            throw new IllegalStateException(...unchanged...);
        }
        return segmentSource.fetch(delivery.grant());
    }

Full 'GATE_SCOPE default ./gradlew test' is GREEN with that applied (verified, exit 0, all modules). Every ConsumerClient built with a SegmentSource lives in DirectFetchTest (lines 134, 157, 180) and every one of them pushes only DIRECT deliveries; every test that pushes INLINE (ConsumerClientTest, BinStoreShardConsumerTest, RestartResumeTest, EndToEndTest) builds the 3-arg ConsumerClient where segmentSource is null. So the 'delivery.via() != FetchMode.DIRECT' branch is EXECUTED constantly and CONSTRAINED never -- testing.md rule 8's exact failure. The bug it lets through is the project's central cost property: with a real source wired at M5.45h, every INLINE delivery -- the default mode, the one that makes zero consumer-side requests hold -- would fetch over the network (and NPE on the null grant in any real fetcher), and testing.md rule 22 makes 'zero requests on the hot path' a test obligation rather than a dashboard. Closing it is one case: build the client WITH a ServesOneUrl, push an INLINE delivery carrying real bytes, assert the records decode AND assert source.asked is empty.

MEASURED SURVIVING MUTATION. Narrow the second guard from the negation to the single value that is tested:

    if (grant != null && via == FetchMode.INLINE) {

Full './gradlew test' is GREEN with that applied (verified, exit 0). aGrantOnANonDIRECTDeliveryIsRefused (DirectFetchTest.java:115) passes FetchMode.INLINE and no test anywhere constructs a client-side Delivery with FetchMode.PROXY -- grep confirms the only PROXY usages are in format and ingest tests. So a PROXY delivery carrying a signed URL is accepted, which is precisely the state security.md rule 4 and the guard's own comment ('a signed URL minted for a consumer that will never fetch with it is one more place it leaks from') exist to forbid: PROXY is the mode where the INGESTER streams the bytes, so the consumer will never fetch, and the secret is pure leak surface. Two lines close it -- a @ParameterizedTest over {INLINE, PROXY}, or a second assertThatThrownBy. (For the record, the message assertions themselves are NOT the weakness: deleting either guard makes assertThatThrownBy fail with 'no exception thrown', so both cases survive guard deletion and only break on rewording.)

MEASURED SURVIVING MUTATION. Memoize the first grant a client ever sees:

    private binjava.format.Grant firstGrantSeen;
    ...
    if (firstGrantSeen == null) { firstGrantSeen = delivery.grant(); }
    return segmentSource.fetch(firstGrantSeen);

':client:test' is GREEN with that applied (verified). Every source-bearing case pushes exactly ONE DIRECT delivery, so 'the ARRIVING grant' and 'the first grant' are indistinguishable and criterion 4's wording is only half-pinned: a client that cached a grant -- a realistic optimisation, grants have a TTL and 'reuse while valid' is the obvious idea -- would serve the first run's object for every later run, which is exactly the 'quietly fetch the wrong object' defect the one-url fake was built to catch. Closing it is one more push in theSEAMIsHandedTheGrantFromTheDELIVERY: a second delivery under a SECOND url, a source that serves both, and containsExactly(urlOne, urlTwo) in order. This matters more at M5.45h, whose whole subject is many deliveries.
